 :adr/id "adr-2608270100-an-alias-cannot-be-checked-against-a-pinned-name"
 :adr/title "An alias cannot be checked against a pinned name"
 :adr/status "accepted"
 :adr/date "2026-08-27"
 :adr/body
  "## The report

Bots はほとんどの実行に失敗していた。CLI と server は正常に見えていた。

## What was measured, 2026-08-27

`cloud.itonami.app.bot-slo.v1` as-of 05:33Z, `status: \"fail\"`.

| window | turns | completed | failed | completion |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| 24h | 150 | 56 | 94 | **37.3%** |
| 7d | 1233 | 693 | 533 | 56.2% |

直近 50 runs の内訳は `model-mismatch 25` / `http-error 8` / `failed 2` /
`completed 10` / `no-op 5`。24h の `provider-timeout` と `tool-budget` は
どちらも 0 なので、当日の支配的な原因は 1 本に絞れた。

`murakumo-main` について、3 つの出所が同時に食い違っていた。

    POST api.murakumo.cloud/v1/chat/completions  model=murakumo-main
      -> HTTP 200, response .model = \"qwen3.8-27b-throughput-b70\"   (5 回連続で同一)
    GET  api.murakumo.cloud/infer/models/murakumo-main
      -> alias-for = \"qwen3.8-27b\"        verified_at 2026-08-24, status \"serving\"
    GET  api.murakumo.cloud/infer/models
      -> どちらの名前も載っていない
         (gemma-4-12b-it / murakumo-main / qwen-agentworld-35b-a3b /
          qwen3.6-35b-a3b / qwen3.6-35b-a3b-xavier-cuda)

app が受理する集合は 1 つだけだった。

    :accepted-response-models {\"murakumo-main\" #{\"murakumo-main\" \"Qwen3.8-27B-Q4_K_M.gguf\"}}

`assert-response-model!` は集合の完全一致しか見ないので、
`\"qwen3.8-27b-throughput-b70\"` は弾かれる。

## The alias has two slots, and they label differently

**この節は当初の記述を訂正する。** 最初の測定では 5 回連続で
`qwen3.8-27b-throughput-b70` が返ったので「安定した 1 つの名前」と書いた。誤りだった。

    06:01Z  1 秒間隔で 3 回 -> murakumo-main / murakumo-main / qwen3.8-27b-throughput-b70
    06:05Z  5 回           -> 両方の名前
    06:07Z  5 回           -> qwen3.8-27b-throughput-b70 のみ

alias は `parallel: 2` を報告する。**2 つのスロットが応答に別々の名前を付けている** ——
片方は要求された alias をそのまま返し、もう片方は自分の deployment id を返す。

これは 24h の完了率が **0% ではなく 37.3%** だったことを説明する。alias を echo する
スロットに当たった turn は admit され、もう片方に当たった turn は拒否されていた。
おおよそトラフィックの分割比である。

**したがって 1 回の probe ではこの問いに答えられない。** 半分の確率で clean を報告し、
その間 production の半分が捨てられている。検出器
（superproject の `scripts/verify-model-alias-admission.cljs`）が 5 回引いて
観測した名前の**集合**を検査するのはこのため。

## Why it read as a model outage

**推論は成功していた。** ゲートウェイは HTTP 200 を返し、本文も正常だった。
捨てていたのは admission である。同じ会話に両方向が残っている。

    [05:37:23Z] bot: 実行に失敗しました。依頼は記録されています。   修正前
    [05:41:24Z] bot: OK                                          修正後

失敗が `provider/*` という名前で記録されるため、fleet 側の障害に見えた。
実際にはこちら側の受理集合が古かった。

## The decision

短期の床として `~/.cloud-itonami/data/config.edn` の provider override に
実測した名前を列挙し、shipped default も同日の実測に合わせる。

**列挙は床であって修正ではない。** `-b70` を動かす redeploy が来れば同じ形で
再発する。alias は定義上「fleet が今指している先」なので、その名前を手元に
literal で固定すること自体が alias の契約と矛盾している。

## The fleet half, traced the same day

The alias did not drift. It was pooled, on purpose, hours earlier.
`local-murakumo` PR #161 (Route murakumo-main across B70 and gad) landed
2026-08-27, and `main-pool-endpoints` now round-robins the alias across two
owned origins. The asymmetry is in one function:

    (defn- body-for-origin
      \"Translate murakumo-main to the model contract of the selected owned origin.\"
      [env endpoint body]
      (if (main-pool-model? (:model body))
        (if (b70-origin? env endpoint)
          (assoc body :model b70-model-id)       ; qwen3.8-27b-throughput-b70
          (assoc body :model default-model-id))  ; qwen3.8-27b
        body))

The REQUEST is translated into each origin's contract. The ANSWER is not
translated back. So a caller who asked for the alias receives, about half the
time, a name that is internal routing rather than the thing it asked for.

Two further facts fell out of reading it:

- The registry record still said \"Two production slots with 262144 tokens per
  slot\". That was written 2026-08-24, before the pool. The slots no longer
  share a window: 262144 and 100000.
- `parallel: 2` in that record is from the same pre-pool note and describes one
  service's slots. It does NOT mean two origins. It was cited once during this
  work as if it were a measurement, and that citation was wrong.

`context` was NOT lowered to the pool minimum. The Worker documents reciprocal
failover, so the larger window may still be reachable through it, and lowering
it would be under-advertising. Whether to keep a mixed-window pool, and what
such an alias should advertise, is a capacity decision and not a correction.

## Why the registry was not corrected

A corrected record was written and not sent. `PUT /infer/models/:id` is gated
by the Worker secret `MURAKUMO_SERVICE_TOKEN`, and:

- it cannot be read back (Cloudflare secrets are write-only);
- the kagi item that was supposed to hold it does not exist, measured
  2026-08-15 and already recorded in the secrets map;
- the same env gates `/infer/runs`, `/infer/spend` and `/v1/slow/*`, so
  reissuing it would 401 every node caller of those routes.

`~/.gftd/murakumo-service-token` is a DIFFERENT token -- `murakumo.cloud`'s
`/x402/ingest`, another Worker -- and was measured returning 401 here.

Rotating a shared credential to land a metadata fix is not a trade worth
making, so the write stopped. The gap became a standing detector finding
instead (`model-alias-pool-undeclared`): while the alias answers to more than
one name and the registry declares none of them, every client's accepted set
is hand-maintained and drifts on the next redeploy.

## What `failed` in the resident outcomes actually means

Worth writing down, because it makes the completion rate read worse than the
fleet is. `resident-outcome` maps `blocked` to `:failed` for resident runs,
and the blocked answers measured after the fix were substantive -- one Bot
reported that the admitted repository contains no customer-support inbox, and
another that no concrete technical blocker was identifiable and the worktree
was clean. Those are Bots that ran correctly and found nothing to act on.

`:completed` and `:no-op` are counted apart on purpose. `blocked` is not, so a
Bot with an honest empty answer lands in the same bucket as a real failure.
**Completion rate is therefore not health.**

`timeout` is a real bound -- the app reports that the model did not answer
within 180 seconds. It is contention, not an undersized envelope. Measured at
the workforce's own budget on 2026-08-27, 1024 output tokens took 18.5s /
19.3s / 52.3s / 18.8s -- 19.6 to 55.5 tok/s, against the 11.4 tok/s the 180s
wall was derived from on 2026-08-19. The 2.8x spread across four identical
calls is the shape of the problem.

## Why the durable fix is not landed here

素直な直し方は「受理集合を呼び出し時に alias から導出する」だが、
**今日それをやっても直らない** —— 上の実測のとおり
`alias-for` が返す `\"qwen3.8-27b\"` は、実際に serve している
`\"qwen3.8-27b-throughput-b70\"` と一致していない。fleet 側の registry が
先に正しくなる必要がある。

guard を alias に対して単に無効化する案は採らない。この guard が守っている
のは名前ではなく `:context-window-tokens` で、alias が窓の狭いモデルへ
張り替わったときに over-budget を防ぐのはこの経路しかない。

したがって直すべき順序は次のとおりで、どれも別の決定である。

1. fleet の registry が自分のプールを申告する（`served-model-names`）。
   **credential が要る** —— 上記 \"Why the registry was not corrected\"。
2. app が alias の識別子と context window を呼び出し時に解決する
3. その後で literal の列挙を落とす

## Current state, 2026-08-27 06:45Z

Landed on default branches:

    cloud-itonami-app     5dae529   shipped defaults + this ADR
                                    (suite: 1932 tests, 11477 assertions,
                                     0 failures, 0 errors)
    cloud-itonami-app     d15b321   ADR corrected to the two-slot mechanism
    com-junkawasaki/root  949a2b41  detector verify-model-alias-admission
    com-junkawasaki/root  8b207003  + the undeclared-pool finding
    west pins advanced twice, both fast-forward, behind=0

NOT in git, and this matters: the operative fix lives in the resident
install's `~/.cloud-itonami/data/config.edn` on this machine -- the widened
accepted set AND `:max-active 2`. Backups sit beside it as
`config.edn.bak-20260827-*`. The shipped defaults carry the accepted set; they
do NOT carry `:max-active`, which is a local capacity choice.
**If that file is reset, the outage returns.**

Verified: the same conversation holds a refusal at 05:37:23Z and `OK` at
05:41:24Z; a second Bot answered `READY` at 06:16:26Z. The detector reproduces
the outage on demand (`--accepted murakumo-main` gives exit 1) and passes
unmodified (exit 0, 75s at load 20.71); self-test 6/6.

NOT verified, and not to be reported as fixed:

- The 24h SLO still reads about 36%. It is a rolling window and 26 pre-fix
  `model-mismatch` runs are still inside it. It needs roughly a day.
- `:max-active 2` has never been exercised: no two resident jobs have been due
  at once since the restart (the outcome window sat still from 06:31Z to
  06:45Z). Whether it helps or worsens `timeout` under contention is
  unmeasured.
- Five Bots sit at `waiting-approval` with `next-run-at` days in the past.
  That is the design -- `:held` counts as active so a second tick is not
  started -- not a stall, but nothing moves them except a person.

Two decisions are open and both belong to a person: what a mixed-window pool
should advertise as its `context`, and whether to reissue
`MURAKUMO_SERVICE_TOKEN` and redistribute it to the nodes (which would make
the registry correctable and end the hand-maintained accepted set).

## What this ADR does not claim

- 7d には別の失敗モードが在る（provider-timeout 212 = 17.2%、
  tool-budget 枯渇 60 = 4.9%）。当日は 0 だが、消えた証拠は無い。
- `workforce_status` の `next-run-at` が 2026-08-23T14:13 を指したままなのは、
  **測定して障害ではないと判った**（当初この行は「未測定」と書いていた）。
  この値は全 job の最小値で、該当する 5 本はいずれも `waiting-approval`。
  `workforce-bot-active?` が `:held` を active と数えて二重 tick を防ぐ設計の
  とおりの停止であり、人が承認するまで動かない。
"}]
